We are starting Phase 4 of the timed AI-assisted development candidate exercise.

You may create documentation files in this phase.

Important boundaries:
- Do NOT modify anything under transcript/.
- Do NOT commit anything. I will review and commit manually at the natural checkpoint.
- Do NOT change game.js, app.js, index.html, or tests unless you discover that the documentation cannot truthfully describe the current implementation because of a real defect.
- Do not add dependencies, tooling, backend code, or network calls.
- This phase is documentation only.

The implementation is already complete and manually validated.

Current implementation facts:

1. Browser-based plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript.
2. No build step.
3. No backend, database, dependency, framework, or network call.
4. 3x3 board.
5. Two human players, X and O.
6. X moves first.
7. Valid moves alternate.
8. Occupied squares cannot be played and rejected attempts do not consume the turn.
9. Normal row/column/diagonal wins terminate immediately.
10. Normal wins have precedence over Komi, including move 9.
11. If the board becomes full without a normal winning line, O wins the Komi tiebreak.
12. Terminal states reject further moves.
13. Play Again resets to an empty board with X to move.
14. UI announces exactly:
    - Player X won
    - Player O won
    - Player O won with Komi

Validated exhaustive results:

- X normal wins: 131184
- O normal wins: 77904
- O Komi wins: 46080
- Draws: 0
- Total terminal legal sequences: 255168
- Maximum legal-move depth: 9
- Visited game-tree nodes: 549946
- Focused deterministic checks: 8/8 PASS
- Node exhaustive execution: PASS
- Chrome exhaustive execution: PASS
- Manual Chrome UI tests: PASS for X win, O win, Komi, occupied-square rejection, Play Again/reset, and post-game locking.

Create:

docs/RULES.md
docs/DESIGN.md

Requirements for docs/RULES.md:

Write it so that a stranger could print the file and correctly play the game without reading the code.

It must clearly state:
- objective;
- board and players;
- X starts;
- legal move definition;
- alternating turns;
- all eight normal tic-tac-toe winning-line categories: rows, columns and diagonals;
- normal victory ends the game immediately;
- occupied-square attempts are not legal moves and do not consume the turn;
- the full-board Komi rule;
- why the Komi rule always awards O on a line-free full board: X has 5 marks and O has 4 because X starts;
- normal win precedence on move 9;
- terminal behavior;
- Play Again/reset behavior.

Avoid implementation details in RULES.md.

Requirements for docs/DESIGN.md:

This is an interview-facing engineering document, not marketing copy.

It must cover all candidate-brief requirements:

1. Reading of the brief
   - Explain that the core challenge was not implementing ordinary tic-tac-toe but defining a deterministic variant that cannot draw and always terminates.
   - Identify underspecified points and document the decisions made.

2. Ambiguities and resolutions
   Include at least:
   - meaning of "always terminates";
   - invalid clicks and user inactivity are not legal game-state progress;
   - move-9 normal-win precedence over Komi;
   - reset semantics;
   - whether to track scores/persistence/AI and why they were omitted.

3. Rule sets considered and rejected
   Include the alternatives actually considered during Phase 1:
   - replay after a conventional draw;
   - randomly select a winner on a full line-free board;
   - continue by moving/replacing existing marks.
   Explain why each was rejected.

Do not invent alternatives that were never discussed.

4. Selected design
   Explain the Komi tiebreak and why it was chosen:
   - deterministic;
   - tiny implementation surface;
   - strict nine-valid-move upper bound;
   - easy to test and defend.
   Acknowledge its balance tradeoff: line-free boards always award O.

5. Termination argument
   Give a clear informal proof:
   - each valid move fills exactly one previously empty square;
   - marks are never removed;
   - there are only nine squares;
   - therefore at most nine valid moves occur;
   - a normal win may terminate earlier;
   - otherwise Komi terminates the game after move 9.

6. No-draw argument
   Explain:
   - every normal line terminates with X or O;
   - if no line exists after nine moves, O_KOMI is terminal;
   - therefore every completed legal game has a winner and DRAW is not an outcome.

7. Exhaustive verification
   Describe the actual validator rather than merely quoting expected values.
   Explain:
   - traversal begins at the empty board;
   - every legal successor is recursively visited;
   - traversal stops immediately at terminal states;
   - no memoization is used because distinct move orders are distinct game sequences;
   - the validator independently checks winning-line geometry rather than simply trusting the engine;
   - successor mutation, parity, terminal successors, depth and outcomes are checked;
   - regression oracles are compared only after traversal.

Include the actual verified results:
- X_WIN 131184
- O_WIN 77904
- O_KOMI 46080
- draws 0
- total 255168
- max depth 9
- visited nodes 549946
- 8 focused checks PASS.

8. Architecture
   Describe:
   - game.js as the DOM-independent single source of truth;
   - immutable game states;
   - index.html as structure/minimal CSS;
   - app.js as UI adapter rendering engine state;
   - tests/exhaustive.js and tests/exhaustive.html;
   - transcript/ as raw AI-development evidence.

Keep this proportional to the small project.

9. AI-assisted development approach
   Briefly describe the actual workflow:
   - requirements analysis before coding;
   - rule decision before implementation;
   - pure engine before UI;
   - exhaustive validation before UI;
   - manual browser validation;
   - natural git checkpoints;
   - raw Codex rollout files preserved.
   Do not pretend the AI worked autonomously or that all generated work was accepted without review.

10. Known limitations / unfinished work
    Be candid.
    Include:
    - no score history;
    - no persistence;
    - no computer opponent;
    - no elaborate visual design;
    - validator is synchronous and may briefly block the browser test tab;
    - game API assumes states produced by createGame/playMove rather than validating arbitrary malicious state objects.
    These are deliberate scope decisions unless genuinely broken.

Do not claim anything that is not supported by the current code or validation record.

Before finishing:
- inspect the implementation and tests so the documentation matches the code;
- inspect git status;
- run git diff --check;
- report the two files created;
- identify anything in the requested documentation that cannot be supported by the repository;
- do not modify transcript/;
- do not commit;
- stop and wait for review.

